stateDiagram-v2
    direction LR

    [*] --> Pending : POST /biometric-update-cases — updateStatus forced to "Pending", requestedAt = now

    Pending --> In_Review : PATCH .../review {status: In_Review}
    Pending --> Approved : PATCH .../review {status: Approved} — accepted, no ordering guard
    Pending --> Rejected : PATCH .../review {status: Rejected} — accepted, no ordering guard

    In_Review --> Approved : PATCH .../review {status: Approved}
    In_Review --> Rejected : PATCH .../review {status: Rejected}

    Approved --> In_Review : PATCH .../review — accepted, the current state is never checked
    Rejected --> Approved : PATCH .../review — accepted, the current state is never checked

    note right of Pending
        This diagram follows the canonical store (ms-configuration,
        /api/v1/biometric-update-cases). Its review endpoint only validates
        the target value against In_Review|Approved|Rejected (zod enum /
        regex); it never reads the current update_status, so any transition
        above is possible. FINGERPRINT cases additionally require
        fingerNumber (400 otherwise). The independent ms-biometric
        implementation (see note on Approved) follows the same transitions
        with no ordering guard either, but skips the fingerNumber check.
    end note

    note right of Approved
        Verified 2026-10 against FULL — NOT a mirror, two fully independent
        implementations with different paths and payloads, never synced:
        - configuration (canonical): Postgres biometric_update_case,
          POST/GET/PUT/PATCH review/DELETE /biometric-update-cases,
          requestedBy mandatory, fingerNumber enforced for FINGERPRINT,
          caller scoped to their own person unless they hold
          biometric.case:review. Soft delete sets deletedAt.
        - biometric (independent, not a mirror): MongoDB
          biometric_update_cases, POST/GET/PATCH review/DELETE
          /biometric/update-request*, no requestedBy field at all, no
          fingerNumber field (FINGERPRINT cases are not validated), only one
          coarse permission gates create/list/review. Soft delete sets
          is_active=false + deleted_at, never removes the document.
        A case created through one path is invisible on the other — see
        SEQ-04. endpoint-matrix.md calls the biometric side a "thin proxy of
        the canonical case" — it is not: there is no HTTP call between the
        two services anywhere in this flow.
    end note

    Pending --> [*] : DELETE (soft delete: deletedAt / is_active=false)
    In_Review --> [*] : DELETE (soft delete)
    Approved --> [*] : DELETE (soft delete)
    Rejected --> [*] : DELETE (soft delete)
